---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const twelveCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Squash and stretch', note: 'Deform volumes with movement — a ball landing flattens, a face turning stretches. It sells weight and life; keep the volume constant.' },
    { math: '2 · Anticipation', note: 'Move backwards before forwards: crouch before the jump, wind up before the punch. The audience is told what is about to happen.' },
    { math: '3 · Staging', note: 'Direct the eye: one clear idea per shot, silhouette-readable poses, nothing important hidden by clutter.' },
    { math: '4 · Straight ahead & pose to pose', note: 'Draw frame-by-frame for fluid chaos; set strong keys and inbetween for controlled arcs. Pros mix both.' },
    { math: '5 · Follow-through & overlapping action', note: 'Nothing stops at once — hair, cloth, tails and jaws keep moving after the body settles, each on its own timing.' },
    { math: '6 · Slow in and slow out', note: 'Few drawings at the ends, many in the middle (or vice versa): spacing, not just positions, creates the feel of mass.' },
    { math: '7 · Arc', note: 'Living things move on curves, not straight lines. Straighten the arc and the motion reads mechanical.' },
    { math: '8 · Secondary action', note: 'Supporting motion — a nervous hand, a swinging bag — enriches the main action without stealing focus.' },
    { math: '9 · Timing', note: 'How many frames an action takes. Timing is characterisation: weight, mood and thought all live in the frame count.' },
    { math: '10 · Exaggeration', note: 'Push reality until it reads: what is true at life-speed is invisible at 24 frames per second.' },
    { math: '11 · Solid drawing', note: 'Form, weight and dimension in every pose — the pose must hold up even as a still.' },
    { math: '12 · Appeal', note: 'The audience must want to watch: charisma in design and in motion, for heroes and villains alike.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/animation/foundational/the-twelve-principles-of-animation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Animation is the art of the interval: between one drawing and the next, the audience's eye
    invents motion. The twelve principles — codified by Disney animators Ollie Johnston and Frank
    Thomas in <em>The Illusion of Life</em> (1981) from the studio's 1930s breakthroughs — are the
    grammar of that invention. Every style from hand-drawn to Pixar follows them, whether it admits
    it or not.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="all twelve, in their canonical order" widget="equationStep" config={twelveCfg} />

  <h2>Spacing is the secret</h2>
  <p>
    Beginners move things; animators space them. A ball dropped with evenly-spaced drawings falls
    like a spreadsheet; space the drawings far apart at speed and close together near impact and it
    gains weight. "Slow in and slow out" is really the physics of momentum drawn as frame
    distribution — timing (how long) and spacing (where between) are the two dials of the craft.
  </p>

  <h2>Anticipation and follow-through make it read</h2>
  <p>
    Two principles do most of the storytelling. Anticipation — the wind-up before every action —
    tells the eye what is coming and makes fast actions legible. Follow-through and overlapping
    action keep the world honest after the main motion ends: nothing alive stops all at once.
    Together they are why animation feels alive rather than mechanical.
  </p>

  <h2>The principles are physics plus psychology</h2>
  <p>
    Half the list is physics (arcs, inertia, momentum) stylised for readability; the other half is
    psychology (staging, appeal, exaggeration) — controlling where the audience looks and what it
    feels. That double parentage is why the principles survived the jump from pencils to pixels
    unchanged: the tools changed, the eye did not.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Johnston &amp; Thomas, <em>The Illusion of Life: Disney Animation</em> (1981); Williams, <em>The Animator's Survival Kit</em>; IGDA curriculum framework.</p>
</TopicPage>
